<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glyndyfrdwy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glyndyfrdwy is a village and former lordship in the Dee valley of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, between Corwen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a>. It was held by descendants of the princes of northern Powys, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>, who took his name from it. There on 16 September 1400 his followers proclaimed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, beginning the last great Welsh revolt against English rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glyndyfrdwy.jpg" alt="Glyndyfrdwy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tooplanx · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glyndyfrdwy_village.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
